export anchor TestHandoff:
    description:
        Once every failed test's fix prompt is printed and the outcome is
        written, the run offers to hand them to an agent, so a failure can go
        straight from the report to a fix. A menu on the terminal lists the
        agentic tools Belay knows, claude, codex, opencode and muse, and No
        agent, which ends the run as before.

        The chosen agent starts in the project root, interactively, with the
        terminal: the same fluency prompt `piton agent` gives it, and all the
        fix prompts as its first message. One prompt is given as it is;
        several are given together, each under a heading that numbers it,
        with a line asking the agent to fix them one after another. Belay
        says how each tool is started with a first message, and the test
        plugin starts it, which is why it declares the tools among its
        processes and the interactive capability.

        The menu only shows when stdin and stdout are terminals and at least
        one test failed; piped, in CI, or when every test passed or could not
        be judged, the run ends without asking. A tool that is not on PATH is
        reported instead of started. The run's exit code is the same whatever
        is chosen: it says how the tests went, not how the agent did.

        After the agent is chosen, a second menu lists its models, with
        Default model first, the same models `piton agent` lists. It is
        skipped when the chosen agent judged the tests and a model was given
        for it, which it then keeps. Cancelling it starts nothing. An agent
        that lists no models gets a note and starts with its default.
